      "source_number": "746",
      "verdict": "solved",
      "task": "Compare all four function-value pairs from fig-52 and identify the decreasing and increasing argument sets.",
      "independent_solution": "The actual styled fig-52 graph has a single bottom turning point at x=0. Moving left to right on the negative-x branch lowers the function value, while moving left to right on the positive-x branch raises it. Thus a) -3.6 < -2.1 < 0 implies f(-2.1) < f(-3.6); b) 0 < 1.3 < 2.8 implies f(1.3) < f(2.8); c) x1 < x2 < 0 implies f(x1) > f(x2); d) 0 < x1 < x2 implies f(x1) < f(x2). The decreasing argument set is x<0, and the increasing argument set is x>0; adjoining x=0 gives the valid maximal closed-ended half-lines (-infinity,0] and [0,infinity), respectively. This is a reading of branch order, not an assumption that the curve is exactly x^2, even, or has a specified minimum height. The stated full domain R remains unchanged."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "747",
      "verdict": "solved",
      "task": "Identify the complete increasing, decreasing and constant time intervals from the cyclist speed-time graph.",
      "independent_solution": "The actual styled graph starts at (t,v)=(0,0), rises to (6,5), remains at v=5 from t=6 to t=14, and then decreases to (16,0). Consequently speed increases on [0,6], decreases on [14,16], and remains constant on [6,14]. The open interiors describe the same phases; the common boundary instants are shared endpoints, not extra phases or unassigned time gaps. The graph's displayed motion ends at t=16, so no further rest interval is inferred from the horizontal coordinate axis. Units are seconds and m/s. The final decreasing arc need not represent uniform deceleration."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "748",
      "verdict": "solved",
      "task": "Construct each straight-line graph and decide whether the function increases or decreases.",
      "independent_solution": "For y = 17x - 2, points (0,-2) and (1,15) determine the straight line; its y-intercept is -2 and its x-intercept is 2/17. For x2 > x1, f(x2)-f(x1) = 17(x2-x1) > 0, so it increases on all R. For y = -2.8x + 1 = -(14/5)x + 1, points (0,1) and (5,-13), or (1,-9/5), determine the line; the x-intercept is 5/14. Its difference is -(14/5)(x2-x1) < 0, so it decreases on all R. Recognizing these completed line relationships would not demonstrate personally constructing the graphs."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "749",
      "verdict": "solved",
      "task": "Classify all six linear or constant functions using the strict definitions.",
      "independent_solution": "8x-5 and 0.01x = x/100 increase on R because their slopes 8 and 1/100 are positive. -3x+10, -49x+1000 and -x/12 decrease on R because their slopes -3, -49 and -1/12 are negative. 0x+5 = 5 is constant, hence neither increasing nor decreasing under blocks 12, 14 and 28: for any x1 < x2 its difference is zero. For each linear expression the exact difference is k(x2-x1), so large or small intercepts have no effect."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "750",
      "verdict": "solved",
      "task": "Construct both quadratic graphs and identify their increasing and decreasing argument sets.",
      "independent_solution": "y = x^2 is an upward-opening parabola with vertex (0,0), symmetry axis x=0, and points (+/-1,1), (+/-2,4). It strictly decreases on (-infinity,0] and strictly increases on [0,infinity). For x1 < x2 <= 0, x2^2-x1^2 = (x2-x1)(x2+x1) < 0; for 0 <= x1 < x2 it is > 0. y = -x^2 reflects that graph across the x-axis, with points (+/-1,-1), (+/-2,-4): it increases on (-infinity,0] and decreases on [0,infinity). The open half-lines have the same direction; including the vertex is mathematically valid. Neither quadratic is globally monotone on R. Graph recognition loses personal graph construction but can retain shape, symmetry, vertex and interval judgments."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "751",
      "verdict": "solved",
      "task": "Construct the modern reciprocal graphs -6/x and 4/x and specify their separate monotonicity intervals.",
      "independent_solution": "Both domains are R excluding 0, with asymptotes x=0 and y=0 and no axis intercepts. For -6/x the negative-x branch is positive (quadrant II) and the positive-x branch negative (quadrant IV), for example (-3,2), (-1,6), (1,-6), (3,-2). On either (-infinity,0) or (0,infinity), x1 < x2 gives f(x2)-f(x1) = 6(x2-x1)/(x1*x2) > 0, so each branch increases. For 4/x, branches lie in quadrants III and I, with (-2,-2), (-1,-4), (1,4), (2,2); the difference is -4(x2-x1)/(x1*x2) < 0 on either half-line, so each branch decreases. Neither is globally increasing or decreasing on the disconnected domain. In particular -1 < 1 but (-6)/(-1)=6 > -6=(-6)/1 refutes global increase of -6/x, and 4/(-1)=-4 < 4=4/1 refutes global decrease of 4/x. Within-branch pairs refute the opposite global direction. Never join the half-lines into a single global monotonicity claim or include x=0."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "752",
      "verdict": "solved",
      "task": "Construct any single-valued graph with exactly domain [-5,7], decreasing on [-5,-2), increasing on (-2,4), and decreasing on (4,7].",
      "independent_solution": "One complete witness is f(x)=-x on [-5,-2), f(-2)=0, f(x)=x on (-2,4), f(4)=0, and f(x)=-x on (4,7]. The outer-piece difference is -(x2-x1)<0 and the middle-piece difference is x2-x1>0 on each specified interval. This graph includes filled points (-5,5), (-2,0), (4,0), (7,-7), and open endpoints (-2,2), (-2,-2), (4,4), (4,-4) for the three line pieces; each x in [-5,7] has exactly one value and there are no values outside that domain. The prescribed open monotonicity intervals place no condition on f(-2) or f(4) beyond their existence as real values. They do not force continuity, endpoint heights, or local extrema at the two transitions. The heights in this witness are a choice, not inferred source givens; many continuous and discontinuous graphs satisfy the source. A proposed graph with holes at -2 or 4 fails the full-domain condition. Graph recognition would lose the ability to independently invent and draw a witness."
